From: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri.3012@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] b43: program the N-PHY RX gain control for radio 2057 rev 8

b43_nphy_gain_ctl_workarounds_rev7() has been an empty stub since N-PHY
rev 7+ support was added, so the RX gain control is left at whatever the
initvals put there: LNA1, LNA2 and TIA gain tables, the CRS minimum power
thresholds, the clip1 low gain codes and the W1 clip thresholds are never
programmed.

Program them for radio 2057 rev 8 in 2.4 GHz, 20 MHz. The register and
table writes, and their order, are taken from an MMIO capture of the
proprietary wl driver 6.30.102.7 running on a BCM6362 (D-Link DSL-3580L).
The capture contains two init sequences, on operating channel 1 and on
operating channel 6, and both program the same values in the same order,
so none of them depends on the channel.

Other radio revisions keep the current behaviour. The vendor driver uses
different gain values per radio revision, and the ones brcmsmac carries
for this code path are from an older release: on radio rev 8 it would
write an LNA1 gain set of 9, 14, 19, 24 and a W1 clip threshold of 13,
where the device is programmed with 8, 13, 18, 25 and 24. Extending this
to another revision needs a capture from hardware that has it.

40 MHz and 5 GHz return early, for two different reasons. The vendor driver
does not use 40 MHz in 2.4 GHz on this board, so checking those values needs
a device that runs 40 MHz. For 5 GHz, b43 has no channel table for radio
2057 rev 8, so the driver cannot tune there in the first place.

Signed-off-by: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri.3012@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
index 22359bd..9cfeef9 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
@@ -2479,7 +2479,46 @@ static void b43_nphy_gain_ctl_workarounds_rev19(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 
 static void b43_nphy_gain_ctl_workarounds_rev7(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 {
-	/* TODO - should depend on phy->rev */
+	struct b43_phy *phy = &dev->phy;
+	static const u8 lna1_gain[] = { 0x08, 0x0d, 0x12, 0x19 };
+	static const u8 lna2_gain[] = { 0xfc, 0x06, 0x0a, 0x0f };
+	static const u8 tia_gain[] = {
+		0xff, 0x00, 0x03, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06,
+	};
+	static const u8 tia_gainbits[] = {
+		0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x03, 0x03, 0x03, 0x03, 0x03, 0x03,
+	};
+
+	/* Values are only known for radio rev 8 in 2 GHz, 20 MHz. */
+	if (phy->radio_rev != 8 || b43_is_40mhz(dev) ||
+	    b43_phy_read(dev, B43_NPHY_BANDCTL) & B43_NPHY_BANDCTL_5GHZ)
+		return;
+
+	/* Set Clip 2 detect */
+	b43_phy_set(dev, B43_NPHY_C1_CGAINI, B43_NPHY_C1_CGAINI_CL2DETECT);
+	b43_phy_set(dev, B43_NPHY_C2_CGAINI, B43_NPHY_C2_CGAINI_CL2DETECT);
+
+	b43_phy_maskset(dev, B43_NPHY_BPHYCRSMINPOWER0, ~0x00FF, 0x46);
+	b43_phy_maskset(dev, B43_NPHY_CRSMINPOWERU0, ~0x00FF, 0x44);
+	b43_phy_maskset(dev, B43_NPHY_CRSMINPOWERL0, ~0x00FF, 0x44);
+
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(0, 8), 4, lna1_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(1, 8), 4, lna1_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(0, 16), 4, lna2_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(1, 16), 4, lna2_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(0, 32), 10, tia_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(1, 32), 10, tia_gain);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(2, 32), 10, tia_gainbits);
+	b43_ntab_write_bulk(dev, B43_NTAB8(3, 32), 10, tia_gainbits);
+
+	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_REV3_C1_CLIP_LOGAIN_A, 0x74);
+	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_REV3_C2_CLIP_LOGAIN_A, 0x74);
+	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_REV3_C1_CLIP_LOGAIN_B, 0x18);
+	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_REV3_C2_CLIP_LOGAIN_B, 0x18);
+
+	/* W1 clip threshold, per core */
+	b43_phy_maskset(dev, 0x300, ~0x003F, 24);
+	b43_phy_maskset(dev, 0x301, ~0x003F, 24);
 }
 
 static void b43_nphy_gain_ctl_workarounds_rev3(struct b43_wldev *dev)

Note per il repo, non per la mailing list:

  - Gate: la funzione e' rev-specifica per nome ed e' chiamata solo dai rev 7+,
    e in testa c'e' l'early return su radio_rev != 8, 40 MHz e 5 GHz. Gli altri
    N-PHY escono prima di scrivere qualunque cosa. Controllato anche con
    reverse-tools/check_patch_gating.py.
  - Verificata con l'harness in test/: applicata al tree, il flow `init`
    riproduce il blocco della cattura op per op, 21 op nello stesso ordine, con
    i payload delle otto table-op identici. Senza la patch quei registri non
    vengono scritti affatto.
  - Attenzione a cosa vuol dire: l'harness compila phy_n.c in userspace con
    stub e warning spenti, NON e' un build del kernel, e il codice non ha mai
    girato sul device. Restano da fare il build come modulo e la misura,
    reports/30-rx-sensitivity.md prima e dopo sullo stesso setup: e' il gate
    di M0 e l'unico modo di sapere se questa patch serviva.
  - Provenienza dei valori, dal trace decodificato
    (router-data/dsl-3580l/opinit-ch1-ch6-bw20.decoded), primo init a canale
    operativo 1; il secondo init a canale 6 ripete gli stessi valori a
    #35491..#35576:
      #685/#686  0x1c, 0x32       |= 0x2000        CL2DETECT per core
      #688       0x289             = 0x46 (m 0xff)  bphy crsmin
      #689       0x283             = 0x44 (m 0xff)  crsmin upper
      #690       0x280             = 0x44 (m 0xff)  crsmin lower
      #691/#697  tbl 0/1 off 0x08  = 08 0d 12 19    LNA1 gain, per core
      #703/#709  tbl 0/1 off 0x10  = fc 06 0a 0f    LNA2 gain (fc = -4)
      #715/#727  tbl 0/1 off 0x20  = ff 00 03 06... TIA gain (ff = -1)
      #739/#751  tbl 2/3 off 0x20  = 00 01 02 03... TIA gain bits
      #764..#767 0x37/0x2ad, 0x38/0x2ae = 0x74, 0x18   clip1 lo gain code
      #769/#770  0x300, 0x301      = 24 (m 0x3f)   W1 clip per core
    Ricostruite con reverse-tools/trace_tables.py, 14 table-op su 14 coerenti
    fra intestazione e dati (indirizzo = (id << 10) | off, conteggio = len).
  - Differenze rispetto a brcmsmac, che era la fonte della prima bozza:
    LNA1 9/14/19/24 contro 8/13/18/25 misurati; W1 clip 13 contro 24; il
    valore intermedio 0x40 su 0x283 non viene scritto affatto; e in 2.4 GHz
    il vendore programma anche LNA2, TIA e i gain bits, che il ramo 2 GHz di
    wlc_phy_workarounds_nphy_gainctrl_2057_rev6 non tocca.
  - NON incluse, di proposito: le table-op a tbl 8 (#771..#789), perche' la
    tabella 8 e' AFECTRL e non gain, quindi appartiene a un altro workaround.
